<h2 id="presentation">Internal selectors and visual settings</h2>

The pinned frontend dispatches 61 selector keys. The public blueprint documentation exposes a subset. The inventory labels source-only selectors, addon compatibility and legacy `ui-action`/`ui-color` aliases explicitly; internal keys are not a recommendation to depend on an undocumented API.

Icon, theme, UI colour, navigation, UI action, clock/date format, time format and areas-display configure presentation. Preserve native previews without executing a configured action. A theme preview must not change equipment. Navigation destinations need valid context, and a proposed action needs a named target and outcome. Local documentation specimens are purpose-specific HTML, not replacements for complex native searchable selectors.

<h2 id="errors">Validation and form recovery</h2>

A required value, incompatible value, missing resource and backend failure need different explanations. Associate field errors with the labelled control; link an error summary to the actual invalid input. Do not clear a password or destructive change silently unless the owning security flow requires it and explains recovery. Optional unset, explicit null and empty string can differ: preserve the schema's semantics.

<h2 id="acceptance">Acceptance checks</h2>

Round-trip every supported selector's value type through the native form. Test missing defaults, multi-select removal, context changes, a vanished saved resource and a backend rejection. Verify no code or action executes during preview/validation. Exercise keyboard focus and error recovery for nested choose/object forms at 320px. Internal keys require pinned-source review and native regression evidence before use.

Sources: [Selectors](https://www.home-assistant.io/docs/blueprint/selectors/), [pinned selector dispatcher](https://github.com/home-assistant/frontend/blob/e32bc0696ebefde323f5b791269ad0c57f3f99d9/src/components/ha-selector/ha-selector.ts), [pinned selector types](https://github.com/home-assistant/frontend/blob/e32bc0696ebefde323f5b791269ad0c57f3f99d9/src/data/selector.ts), and [automation editing](automation-editing.html#ha-ctrl-009-capabilities).

<h2 id="evidence">Implementation and evidence boundary</h2>

This is a reference design specification, not a replacement for native Home Assistant components. The examples use invented records and local draft feedback; they do not call Home Assistant. Mapping a registry entry here establishes a design owner, not proof that every integration, browser or device has been tested. Keep native state formatting, translations, permissions, capabilities and event handling. Theme tokens can change presentation; they cannot supply missing application behaviour.

The [interface coverage inventory](../reference/ha-interface-coverage.json) records the finite source baseline and exact mappings. Recheck native behaviour after frontend changes. Use the [native task acceptance process](native-task-accessibility.html) to record what was actually tested.

Apply the [brand rules](../styles/brand.html), [writing guide](../content/index.html), [layout](../styles/layout.html), [colour roles](../styles/colour.html) and [typography](../styles/typography.html). Structural dividers use the quiet border token; editable boundaries and focus indicators remain distinct. Preserve 16px native control text, visible labels, keyboard access and readable names at 320px. A compact arrangement must not remove a capability explanation or essential recovery route.

